# The `squash-layers` tool (design/stacks.md, "Publishing"): the
# std/git-runner image with this directory's script curried onto it, plus the
# `help` it is described by. It returns a VALUE — a tree of new commits — and
# changes no tree, so it is not a `@writer`; the agent links the tree it returns.
HELP=<<END
Squash each layer of a stack into one commit, for publishing each layer as a
branch with a linear history. The layers keep their own merge history; this only
mints new commits beside them.

The layers are the files in `messages`, in byte order (LC_ALL=C): one per layer to
publish, named after its entry in `stack`, holding that layer's full commit
message (subject, blank line, body), used verbatim. An entry with no message
file, such as 00-base, is not published.

For each layer in order, C = commit(tree = the layer tip's tree, parent = the
previous C, or `onto` for the first layer, message = that layer's file), with the
author and committer copied from the layer tip. The same input always mints the
same commits. Refused, naming both, unless each layer's tip already contains the
one below it (or `onto`, for the first): merge a changed lower layer up first.

Returns one tree of gitlinks, {<entry>: <C>}, shown as `result tree <T>: <entries>`.
Link the whole stack with one shell call that declares paths ["publish/<stack>"]
(a republish cannot replace the old link otherwise), then publish_source each
publish/<stack>/<entry> bottom to top with rewrite=true:
  caos get-hash <T> /cas/s; rm -rf publish/<stack>; mkdir -p publish; ln -s /cas/s publish/<stack>
@param {tree} stack Conversation path of the directory holding the layer gitlinks, e.g. `feature`.
@param {commit} onto Full hash of the commit the first layer is published onto: the base branch's tip that layer 1 last merged.
@param {tree} messages Conversation path of a directory holding one commit-message file per layer to publish, named after its stack entry, e.g. `feature-messages` holding `01-core` and `02-tests`.
END
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/git-runner --worker1:@=worker.sh --help=$HELP
